00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:14,480
Then we would have to ask a much bigger question.

29
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,600
Did a pattern fail, or did something in the market

30
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:19,200
itself change?

31
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:23,220
TradeWave is a historical analytics machine.

32
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:27,234
It is a map of what happened in comparable periods.

33
00:01:27,234 --> 00:01:30,967
It cannot promise where we will end up, but it can

34
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:34,021
show when the market has moved beyond every previous

35
00:01:34,021 --> 00:01:34,700
winning path.

36
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,728
Here is the rule. The window runs from September

37
00:01:39,728 --> 00:01:42,213
twenty-seventh of a United States midterm-election

38
00:01:42,213 --> 00:01:45,526
year through July eighteenth of the following year,

39
00:01:45,526 --> 00:01:48,840
a total of two hundred ninety-five calendar days.

40
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,685
Because both dates fall on Sunday this cycle,

41
00:01:53,685 --> 00:01:57,015
TradeWave records the next trading closes: Monday,

42
00:01:57,015 --> 00:01:59,393
September twenty-eighth, and Monday, July

43
00:01:59,393 --> 00:02:00,820
nineteenth, twenty twenty-seven.

44
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,000
The dates are fixed before we know the result. No

45
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,400
moving the goalposts later.

46
00:02:08,040 --> 00:02:10,802
When TradeWave first produced this chart, almost

47
00:02:10,802 --> 00:02:12,380
every bar was green.

48
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:16,320
My first thought was not, I found something.

49
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:20,120
My first thought was, Something must be wrong.

50
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,094
I saved it, moved on, and opened it again about an

51
00:02:25,094 --> 00:02:28,280
hour later. Then I checked it again. Same result.

52
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:32,588
These were not twenty-three years in a row. They

53
00:02:32,588 --> 00:02:34,767
were twenty-three separate midterm cycles spread

54
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:36,220
across almost a century.

55
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:39,513
Yet the same window finished higher twenty-three

56
00:02:39,513 --> 00:02:40,960
times out of twenty-four.

57
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:44,993
That investigation eventually became my book, The

58
00:02:44,993 --> 00:02:46,600
